@sen156     Alphonse de Candolle and others have shown that plants which have very wide ranges generally present varieties; and this might have been expected, as they are exposed to diverse physical conditions, and as they come into competition which, as we shall hereafter see, is a far more important circumstance with different sets of organic beings.

              " (extract-fr) part 3 of Darwin about: each other, natural selection, incipient species, well-marked varieties, general rule, hereafter see, one side, permanent varieties, smaller genera, any country, larger genera, other side, equal masses, struggle for existence, divergence of character, have been formed, rank of species

@sen157    But my tables further show that, in any limited country, the species which are the most common, that is abound most in individuals, and the species which are most widely diffused within their own country and this is a different consideration from wide range, and to a certain extent from commonness, oftenest give rise to varieties sufficiently well-marked to have been recorded in botanical works.

              " (extract-fr) part 3 of Darwin about: each other, natural selection, incipient species, well-marked varieties, general rule, hereafter see, one side, permanent varieties, smaller genera, any country, larger genera, other side, equal masses, struggle for existence, divergence of character, have been formed, rank of species

@sen158    Hence it is the most flourishing, or, as they may be called, the dominant species--those which range widely, are the most diffused in their own country, and are the most numerous in individuals--which oftenest produce well-marked varieties, or, as I consider them, incipient species.

              " (extract-fr) part 3 of Darwin about: each other, natural selection, incipient species, well-marked varieties, general rule, hereafter see, one side, permanent varieties, smaller genera, any country, larger genera, other side, equal masses, struggle for existence, divergence of character, have been formed, rank of species

@sen159    And this, perhaps, might have been anticipated; for, as varieties, in order to become in any degree permanent, necessarily have to struggle with the other inhabitants of the country, the species which are already dominant will be the most likely to yield offspring, which, though in some slight degree modified, still inherit those advantages that enabled their parents to become dominant over their compatriots.

              " (extract-fr) part 3 of Darwin about: each other, natural selection, incipient species, well-marked varieties, general rule, hereafter see, one side, permanent varieties, smaller genera, any country, larger genera, other side, equal masses, struggle for existence, divergence of character, have been formed, rank of species

@sen160    In these remarks on predominence, it should be understood that reference is made only to the forms which come into competition with each other, and more especially to the members of the same genus or class having nearly similar habits of life.

