@sen177    Where many species of a genus have been formed through variation, circumstances have been favourable for variation; and hence we might expect that the circumstances would generally still be favourable to variation.

              " (extract-fr) part 3 of Darwin about: each other, natural selection, incipient species, well-marked varieties, general rule, hereafter see, one side, permanent varieties, smaller genera, any country, larger genera, other side, equal masses, struggle for existence, divergence of character, have been formed, rank of species

@sen178    On the other hand, if we look at each species as a special act of creation, there is no apparent reason why more varieties should occur in a group having many species, than in one having few.

