@sen481    In practice, a fancier is, for instance, struck by a pigeon having a slightly shorter beak; another fancier is struck by a pigeon having a rather longer beak; and on the acknowledged principle that "fanciers do not and will not admire a medium standard, but like extremes," they both go on as has actually occurred with the sub-breeds of the tumbler-pigeon choosing and breeding from birds with longer and longer beaks, or with shorter and shorter beaks.

