@sen985    But it will have been otherwise with the nascent giraffe, considering its probable habits of life; for those individuals which had some one part or several parts of their bodies rather more elongated than usual, would generally have survived.

              " (extract-fr) part 21 of Darwin about: other hand, has remarked, each other, natural selection, each species, differ much, nascent giraffe, lofty stature, other object, branches of trees

@sen986    These will have intercrossed and left offspring, either inheriting the same bodily peculiarities, or with a tendency to vary again in the same manner; while the individuals less favoured in the same respects will have been the most liable to perish.

              " (extract-fr) part 21 of Darwin about: other hand, has remarked, each other, natural selection, each species, differ much, nascent giraffe, lofty stature, other object, branches of trees

@sen987     We here see that there is no need to separate single pairs, as man does, when he methodically improves a breed: natural selection will preserve and thus separate all the superior individuals, allowing them freely to intercross, and will destroy all the inferior individuals.

