              " (extract-fr) part 34 of Darwin about: south america, organic beings, united states, consecutive formations, years ago, secondary periods, oscillations of level, our consecutive formations

@sen1567     From these considerations, from our ignorance of the geology of other countries beyond the confines of Europe and the United States, and from the revolution in our palaeontological knowledge effected by the discoveries of the last dozen years, it seems to me to be about as rash to dogmatize on the succession of organic forms throughout the world, as it would be for a naturalist to land for five minutes on a barren point in Australia, and then to discuss the number and range of its productions.

