@sen1624    We can perhaps understand the apparently quicker rate of change in terrestrial and in more highly organised productions compared with marine and lower productions, by the more complex relations of the higher beings to their organic and inorganic conditions of life, as explained in a former chapter.

              " (extract-fr) part 36 of Darwin about: south america, natural selection, north america, different climates, extinct species, other mammals, tertiary formations, large sense, throughout the world, forms of life, varieties and species, groups of species, most different climates, rich in fossils, from the world

@sen1625    When many of the inhabitants of any area have become modified and improved, we can understand, on the principle of competition, and from the all-important relations of organism to organism in the struggle for life, that any form which did not become in some degree modified and improved, would be liable to extermination.

