@sen1638     We have seen in the last chapter that whole groups of species sometimes falsely appear to have been abruptly developed; and I have attempted to give an explanation of this fact, which if true would be fatal to my views.

              " (extract-fr) part 36 of Darwin about: south america, natural selection, north america, different climates, extinct species, other mammals, tertiary formations, large sense, throughout the world, forms of life, varieties and species, groups of species, most different climates, rich in fossils, from the world

@sen1639    But such cases are certainly exceptional; the general rule being a gradual increase in number, until the group reaches its maximum, and then, sooner or later, a gradual decrease.

              " (extract-fr) part 36 of Darwin about: south america, natural selection, north america, different climates, extinct species, other mammals, tertiary formations, large sense, throughout the world, forms of life, varieties and species, groups of species, most different climates, rich in fossils, from the world

@sen1640    If the number of the species included within a genus, or the number of the genera within a family, be represented by a vertical line of varying thickness, ascending through the successive geological formations, in which the species are found, the line will sometimes falsely appear to begin at its lower end, not in a sharp point, but abruptly; it then gradually thickens upwards, often keeping of equal thickness for a space, and ultimately thins out in the upper beds, marking the decrease and final extinction of the species.

              " (extract-fr) part 36 of Darwin about: south america, natural selection, north america, different climates, extinct species, other mammals, tertiary formations, large sense, throughout the world, forms of life, varieties and species, groups of species, most different climates, rich in fossils, from the world

@sen1641    This gradual increase in number of the species of a group is strictly conformable with the theory; for the species of the same genus, and the genera of the same family, can increase only slowly and progressively; the process of modification and the production of a number of allied forms necessarily being a slow and gradual process, one species first giving rise to two or three varieties, these being slowly converted into species, which in their turn produce by equally slow steps other varieties and species, and so on, like the branching of a great tree from a single stem, till the group becomes large.

              " (extract-fr) part 36 of Darwin about: south america, natural selection, north america, different climates, extinct species, other mammals, tertiary formations, large sense, throughout the world, forms of life, varieties and species, groups of species, most different climates, rich in fossils, from the world

@sen1643    On the theory of natural selection, the extinction of old forms and the production of new and improved forms are intimately connected together.

              " (extract-fr) part 36 of Darwin about: south america, natural selection, north america, different climates, extinct species, other mammals, tertiary formations, large sense, throughout the world, forms of life, varieties and species, groups of species, most different climates, rich in fossils, from the world

@sen1646    On the contrary, we have every reason to believe, from the study of the tertiary formations, that species and groups of species gradually disappear, one after another, first from one spot, then from another, and finally from the world.

