@sen2120    Afterwards they undergo a complete change; their eyes disappear; their legs and antennae become rudimentary, and they feed on honey; so that they now more closely resemble the ordinary larvae of insects; ultimately they undergo a further transformation, and finally emerge as the perfect beetle.

              " (extract-fr) part 47 of Darwin about: same species, organic beings, allied species, other hand, natural selection, rudimentary organs, same class, natural system, our theory, long period, embryonic condition, corresponding period, perfect state, nascent condition, quite useless, habits of life, closely allied species, period of life, state of things, facts in embryology

@sen2121    Now, if an insect, undergoing transformations like those of the Sitaris, were to become the progenitor of a whole new class of insects, the course of development of the new class would be widely different from that of our existing insects; and the first larval stage certainly would not represent the former condition of any adult and ancient form.

              " (extract-fr) part 47 of Darwin about: same species, organic beings, allied species, other hand, natural selection, rudimentary organs, same class, natural system, our theory, long period, embryonic condition, corresponding period, perfect state, nascent condition, quite useless, habits of life, closely allied species, period of life, state of things, facts in embryology

@sen2122     On the other hand it is highly probable that with many animals the embryonic or larval stages show us, more or less completely, the condition of the progenitor of the whole group in its adult state.

              " (extract-fr) part 47 of Darwin about: same species, organic beings, allied species, other hand, natural selection, rudimentary organs, same class, natural system, our theory, long period, embryonic condition, corresponding period, perfect state, nascent condition, quite useless, habits of life, closely allied species, period of life, state of things, facts in embryology

@sen2123    In the great class of the Crustacea, forms wonderfully distinct from each other, namely, suctorial parasites, cirripedes, entomostraca, and even the malacostraca, appear at first as larvae under the nauplius-form; and as these larvae live and feed in the open sea, and are not adapted for any peculiar habits of life, and from other reasons assigned by Fritz Muller, it is probable that at some very remote period an independent adult animal, resembling the Nauplius, existed, and subsequently produced, along several divergent lines of descent, the above-named great Crustacean groups.

              " (extract-fr) part 47 of Darwin about: same species, organic beings, allied species, other hand, natural selection, rudimentary organs, same class, natural system, our theory, long period, embryonic condition, corresponding period, perfect state, nascent condition, quite useless, habits of life, closely allied species, period of life, state of things, facts in embryology

@sen2124    So again, it is probable, from what we know of the embryos of mammals, birds, fishes and reptiles, that these animals are the modified descendants of some ancient progenitor, which was furnished in its adult state with branchiae, a swim-bladder, four fin-like limbs, and a long tail, all fitted for an aquatic life.

              " (extract-fr) part 47 of Darwin about: same species, organic beings, allied species, other hand, natural selection, rudimentary organs, same class, natural system, our theory, long period, embryonic condition, corresponding period, perfect state, nascent condition, quite useless, habits of life, closely allied species, period of life, state of things, facts in embryology

@sen2125     As all the organic beings, extinct and recent, which have ever lived, can be arranged within a few great classes; and as all within each class have, according to our theory, been connected together by fine gradations, the best, and, if our collections were nearly perfect, the only possible arrangement, would be genealogical; descent being the hidden bond of connexion which naturalists have been seeking under the term of the Natural System.

              " (extract-fr) part 47 of Darwin about: same species, organic beings, allied species, other hand, natural selection, rudimentary organs, same class, natural system, our theory, long period, embryonic condition, corresponding period, perfect state, nascent condition, quite useless, habits of life, closely allied species, period of life, state of things, facts in embryology

@sen2126    On this view we can understand how it is that, in the eyes of most naturalists, the structure of the embryo is even more important for classification than that of the adult.

              " (extract-fr) part 47 of Darwin about: same species, organic beings, allied species, other hand, natural selection, rudimentary organs, same class, natural system, our theory, long period, embryonic condition, corresponding period, perfect state, nascent condition, quite useless, habits of life, closely allied species, period of life, state of things, facts in embryology

@sen2127    In two or more groups of animals, however much they may differ from each other in structure and habits in their adult condition, if they pass through closely similar embryonic stages, we may feel assured that they are all descended from one parent-form, and are therefore closely related.

              " (extract-fr) part 47 of Darwin about: same species, organic beings, allied species, other hand, natural selection, rudimentary organs, same class, natural system, our theory, long period, embryonic condition, corresponding period, perfect state, nascent condition, quite useless, habits of life, closely allied species, period of life, state of things, facts in embryology

@sen2128    Thus, community in embryonic structure reveals community of descent; but dissimilarity in embryonic development does not prove discommunity of descent, for in one of two groups the developmental stages may have been suppressed, or may have been so greatly modified through adaptation to new habits of life as to be no longer recognisable.

              " (extract-fr) part 47 of Darwin about: same species, organic beings, allied species, other hand, natural selection, rudimentary organs, same class, natural system, our theory, long period, embryonic condition, corresponding period, perfect state, nascent condition, quite useless, habits of life, closely allied species, period of life, state of things, facts in embryology

@sen2129    Even in groups, in which the adults have been modified to an extreme degree, community of origin is often revealed by the structure of the larvae; we have seen, for instance, that cirripedes, though externally so like shell-fish, are at once known by their larvae to belong to the great class of crustaceans.

              " (extract-fr) part 47 of Darwin about: same species, organic beings, allied species, other hand, natural selection, rudimentary organs, same class, natural system, our theory, long period, embryonic condition, corresponding period, perfect state, nascent condition, quite useless, habits of life, closely allied species, period of life, state of things, facts in embryology

@sen2130    As the embryo often shows us more or less plainly the structure of the less modified and ancient progenitor of the group, we can see why ancient and extinct forms so often resemble in their adult state the embryos of existing species of the same class.

